VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       POSIX.1‐2001  says  that  lliinnkk()  should dereference _o_l_d_p_a_t_h if it is a
       symbolic link.  However, since Linux 2.0, Linux does not do so: if _o_l_d_‐
       _p_a_t_h is a symbolic link, then _n_e_w_p_a_t_h is created as a  (hard)  link  to
       the  same  symbolic link file (i.e., _n_e_w_p_a_t_h becomes a symbolic link to
       the same file that _o_l_d_p_a_t_h refers to).  Some other implementations  be‐
       have  in the same manner as Linux.  POSIX.1‐2008 changes the specifica‐
       tion of lliinnkk(), making it implementation‐dependent whether or not  _o_l_d_‐
       _p_a_t_h  is  dereferenced  if  it is a symbolic link.  For precise control
       over the  treatment  of  symbolic  links  when  creating  a  link,  use
       lliinnkkaatt().

   gglliibbcc
       On older kernels where lliinnkkaatt() is unavailable, the glibc wrapper func‐
       tion  falls  back to the use of lliinnkk(), unless the AATT__SSYYMMLLIINNKK__FFOOLLLLOOWW is
       specified.  When _o_l_d_p_a_t_h and _n_e_w_p_a_t_h are relative pathnames, glibc con‐
       structs pathnames based on the symbolic  links  in  _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_e_l_f_/_f_d  that
       correspond to the _o_l_d_d_i_r_f_d and _n_e_w_d_i_r_f_d arguments.

